## Symptom

Any request whose body contains a non-ASCII character dies before it reaches the
cluster, with an error that names neither this distribution nor the offending
data:

```
HTTP::Message content must be bytes at Kubernetes/REST/LWPIO.pm line 68
```

Hit in the wild by OCP applying the upstream cert-manager manifest
(`v1.21.1`, 17 non-ASCII bytes in the CRD descriptions — `§`, `–`).

## Cause

`Kubernetes/REST.pm:93`

```perl
has _json => (is => 'ro', default => sub { JSON::MaybeXS->new });
```

No `utf8 => 1`, so `->encode` returns a character string. `_build_request`
(`REST.pm:461`) hands that straight to `HTTPRequest->content`, and
`HTTP::Request` refuses anything with a character above U+00FF.

The chain, each step verified:

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| `HTTP::Tiny` fetches cert-manager.yaml | bytes (`application/octet-stream`, no decode) |
| `YAML::XS::Load` | **character** strings — YAML::XS decodes UTF-8 by design |
| `$self->_json->encode($body)` | character string ← the defect |
| `HTTP::Request->content` | dies |

`JSON::MaybeXS->new(utf8 => 1)->encode` on the same structure returns bytes and
is accepted.

## Second defect in the same area

The two IO backends disagree about what `HTTPResponse->content` holds, and one
of them contradicts IO::K8s:

- `LWPIO.pm:118,148` — `$res->decoded_content` → **characters**
- `HTTPTinyIO.pm:110,138` — `$res->{content}` → **bytes** (HTTP::Tiny only
  decodes `text/*`, and the API answers `application/json`)

`_inflate_object` passes that content to `IO::K8s::json_to_object`, and IO::K8s
decodes with `JSON::MaybeXS->new(utf8 => 1, ...)` (`IO/K8s.pm:111`) — it expects
bytes. So under the LWP backend, a response containing non-ASCII gets its
already-decoded characters decoded a second time: mojibake, silently, no error.
Under the HTTP::Tiny backend the same call is correct. `_inflate_list`
(`REST.pm:481`) decodes via `_json` and inherits the same split.

Worth confirming with a test that runs the same non-ASCII payload through both
backends and asserts identical objects — nothing currently pins that.

## Suggested direction

Standardise on bytes end to end, which is what IO::K8s already assumes:

1. `_json` → `JSON::MaybeXS->new(utf8 => 1)` — fixes encode and lines decode up
   with IO::K8s.
2. `LWPIO` → `$res->content` instead of `$res->decoded_content`, so both
   backends hand back the same thing.

Check while doing it: `_check_response` (`REST.pm:469`) interpolates
`$response->content` into a `croak` message, so an error body with non-ASCII
becomes a "Wide character"-free but mis-rendered string — decide whether that
one wants an explicit decode for human output.

## Reproduce

```perl
use Kubernetes::REST;
# any object with a non-ASCII value, e.g.
my $cm = { apiVersion => 'v1', kind => 'ConfigMap',
           metadata => { name => 'utf8-probe', namespace => 'default' },
           data => { note => "caf\x{e9} \x{a7}" } };
# -> HTTP::Message content must be bytes
```

Found while debugging `ocp apply` against a live RKE2 cluster; OCP has no
workaround, cert-manager setup simply fails there.
Fixed in the working tree (uncommitted).

- REST.pm: _json is now JSON::MaybeXS->new(utf8 => 1, canonical => 1, convert_blessed => 1) -- encode() emits bytes, decode() lines up with IO::K8s.
- LWPIO: both call() and call_streaming() go through a shared _response(), using decoded_content(charset => 'none'). Content-Encoding is undone, the charset is not. Matches HTTPTinyIO.
- _check_response: error body is UTF-8 decoded (FB_DEFAULT) before croak, so a non-ASCII API error is readable and a malformed body cannot turn an API error into an encoding death.
- CLI.pm: output encoder gained ->utf8, otherwise the now correctly-decoded objects would hit print as wide characters. CLI/Watch.pm already had it.
- t/lib/Test/Kubernetes/Mock.pm: the mock IO backend now returns bytes like the real ones do.
- log() deliberately stays bytes -- container output is not guaranteed to be text, let alone UTF-8. Documented, with the decode one-liner.

Docs: encoding contract in Role::IO (custom backends must not decode), new ENCODING section in Kubernetes::REST, note in each backend, Changes entry.

